I’ll inspect the metadata, diffraction data, and reusable workflows first, then build a candidate inventory and carry the accepted model through GSAS-II refinement and export verification. I’ll treat weak candidates and alternate polymorphs explicitly, and I’ll only report fractions supported by a replayed final project.

The chemical prior is PbO–WO₃ reacted at 500 °C, but I won’t assume complete formation of PbWO₄. I’m now checking the raw peak inventory and the available database/indexing/refinement interfaces so that residual precursor or intermediate phases remain testable.

The peak families already separate into three coherent candidates: scheelite-type PbWO₄ dominates; orthorhombic PbO accounts for the strong 29.35°/30.62° family; and room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the 23.39°/23.89°/24.64° triplet. I’m screening alternate records and polymorphs now—especially raspite PbWO₄, litharge PbO, and the competing WO₃ settings—before any fraction is trusted.

The three-phase model is stable and all three scales remain positive after cell refinement. Its current Rwp is about 23.3%, with the remaining mismatch dominated by peak-shape/intensity details rather than missing peak positions. I’m now establishing the common profile, then I’ll re-run the polymorph watch list under that same profile.

The watch-list replay found a fourth phase: tetragonal litharge PbO is retained at roughly 3 wt%, supported by its 29.0°, 32.1°, 35.95°, 48.86°, 54.91°, and 60.03° family. Adding it lowers Rwp from 17.47% to 16.94%, while raspite PbWO₄ collapses to zero. I’m rebuilding the shared profile for this four-phase inventory before testing phase-specific broadening or texture.

The profile audit rejects isotropic phase broadening: PbWO₄ and WO₃ terms are negligible, while PbO size/strain trials run to limits and distort the fractions. The residual families are therefore being treated as an inventory/anisotropy question, not absorbed into broad peaks. I’m checking oxidized lead phases and then the symmetry-appropriate massicot broadening directions.

A fifth phase is supported: room-temperature minium Pb₃O₄ at about 3.2 wt%. Its strongest independent family accounts for residuals near 26.76°, 31.05°, 34.43°, 50.22°, and 52.1°, and lowers Rwp to 15.91% without a bound scale. I’m rebuilding the common W/X and polarization model again, then I’ll test whether any further oxidized/intermediate phase has a nonzero, coherent contribution.

The Pb₂O₃ trial produces a large residual reduction, so I’m treating it as a serious inventory candidate rather than accepting it on fit quality alone. I’m now checking its refined fraction/cell and unique reflection families, then I’ll challenge it against chemically plausible lead-rich tungstate prototypes under the same profile model.

The analogue search found a particularly strong chemical match: Pb₂MoO₅ (C2/m), where Mo⁶⁺→W⁶⁺ is an isovalent, closely size-matched substitution. I also inspected Pb₂CrO₅ and Pb₂TeO₅ parents; the Mo parent is the defensible first prototype. I’m adding that substituted model as a trial phase and will compare it directly with Pb₂O₃—not simply stack both into the refinement.

The geometry screen changes the interpretation: the COD Pb₂O₃ record contains a 1.34 Å Pb–O contact even before refinement, which is chemically impossible. Its apparent improvement is therefore not acceptable evidence for Pb₂O₃; it is a pattern-matching surrogate. I’m checking alternate Pb₂O₃ records before rejecting the phase, while the Pb₂WO₅ prototype passes the same screen (tetrahedral W–O 1.76–1.79 Å and a cell within about 1% of its Mo analogue).

The physically valid Pb₂O₃ alternate goes to the nonnegative scale bound, so Pb₂O₃ is rejected. By contrast, the Pb₃WO₆ U⁶⁺→W⁶⁺ prototype remains at roughly 7 wt%, improves the fit even with its cell fixed, and has plausible octahedral W–O and Pb–O geometry. I’m provisionally retaining it and rebuilding the common background, source, and profile model for the seven-phase inventory before testing widths and texture.

The rebuilt seven-phase baseline is stable on reopen (Rwp ≈ 11.78%) with positive interior U/W/X values and polarization ≈ 0.833. The one-phase-at-a-time width screen is underway; early size trials are moving to bounds or worsening the fit, while I’m checking whether any strain term is both interior and replay-stable before retaining it.

The width results confirm the baseline diagnosis: every crystallite-size trial runs to a bound or degrades the fit. Massicot and litharge strain terms also run to the imposed maximum despite lowering Rwp, so they are being rejected as surrogates rather than interpreted physically. The weaker WO₃/Pb₂WO₅ strain changes are small and still need replay/bound checks; no width term is accepted yet.

The texture screen has found one credible candidate so far: massicot with a [100] March–Dollase axis (ratio ≈ 0.81), which lowers Rwp by about 0.12 absolute; other massicot axes are clearly weaker, and all tested stolzite axes stay essentially at 1.0. I’ll only keep [100] if its improvement survives combination/replay and is visible across more than one massicot reflection family.

A second strong texture candidate has emerged for the Pb₂WO₅ analogue: [100] gives an interior MD ratio near 1.20 and lowers Rwp by about 0.28 absolute. WO₃ axes remain effectively neutral. I’m finishing the diagonal-axis comparisons, then I’ll combine only the best-supported massicot and Pb₂WO₅ terms and replay from a fresh reopen.

Pb₃WO₆ also shows strong axis dependence ([100] currently best, MD ≈ 0.64), but because this is a substituted U-based prototype I’m treating that cautiously: a large texture correction can mask imperfect prototype intensities. I’ll require coherent support across several phase-specific families and will report the prototype-derived nature explicitly if it remains in the final model.

After texture is included, Pb₂WO₅ isotropic microstrain remains interior (≈3,030 µstrain) and replay-worthy, reducing Rwp from about 10.02 to 9.93. Pb₃WO₆ strain again runs to the 5,000-µstrain bound and changes its scale sharply, so that term is rejected. I’m now challenging the Pb₂WO₅ broadening with monoclinic uniaxial alternatives before deciding whether the simpler isotropic term is adequate.

The monoclinic anisotropy challenge does not justify the extra parameter: [100] is the best fully interior trial, but improves Rwp by only ~0.012 and [101] is nearly equivalent; the nominally better diagonal axes hit bounds. I’m retaining the simpler isotropic Pb₂WO₅ microstrain, rebuilding the common profile around it, and replaying from disk.

The final baseline now has replay-stable common widths and asymmetry across isolated low-, mid-, and high-angle families; the remaining errors are mainly intensity/systematic model errors rather than a consistent peak-position or FWHM trend. I’m doing the required final watch-list re-challenge now (raspite PbWO₄, PbO₂, and a physically valid Pb₂O₃ analogue) under this exact common model.

The final watch-list result is clean: raspite and the physical Pb₂O₃ alternate return to zero scale. PbO₂ refines to only ~0.39 wt%, but its nominally unique strongest lines do not show a coherent positive residual family and PbO₂ is thermally implausible after 500 °C treatment, so I am not modeling it as a reliable phase. I’m now replaying the accepted seven-phase model into `final.gpx` and exporting every submitted derivative from that persisted project.

A final export validation caught an important issue before handoff: the COD litharge file’s origin-1 setting was converted by GSAS-II into the wrong Pb:O multiplicity (Pb₂O). I’m correcting that phase to the standard origin-2 P4/nmm PbO setting and will rerun the refinement and all exports; the current `final.gpx`/results are not being treated as final until this check passes.

I found and corrected an origin-choice error in the litharge reference that would otherwise export the wrong Pb:O stoichiometry. I’m checking the replayed refinement now, then I’ll revalidate every exported structure and regenerate the final project and reports from the corrected model.

The corrected litharge model remains independently supported at about 3.8 wt% and the seven-phase fit replays cleanly (Rwp ≈ 10.00%). Its changed intensities modestly affect the nuisance parameters, so I’m re-running the phase-specific width/texture challenges under this corrected common profile before freezing the deliverables.

The corrected-model challenges are decisive: litharge texture gives only a negligible gain, while its size/strain trials run into bounds, so those terms stay neutral. Raspite and a physically plausible Pb₂O₃ prototype refine to zero. A PbO₂ trial gives a small numerical gain but fails its strongest-family consistency check and is chemically incompatible with PbO/WO₃ heated at 500 °C, so I’m treating it as an unreliable trace rather than reporting a fraction.

The accepted model has now been replayed from disk with a maximum shift/esd of 0.014, and all requested outputs were regenerated from that persisted project. I’m doing the last integrity pass now: CIF stoichiometry/symmetry/geometry, fraction normalization, and exact agreement between `final.gpx`, `result.json`, and `fit.csv`.

Completed the seven-phase refinement. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material:

| Phase | wt% |
|---|---:|
| PbWO₄, stolzite | 58.04(21) |
| Pb₂WO₅, C2/m prototype | 16.07(23) |
| WO₃, monoclinic | 9.05(14) |
| Pb₃WO₆, Pnam prototype | 6.06(23) |
| PbO, massicot | 5.55(11) |
| PbO, litharge | 3.83(8) |
| Pb₃O₄, minium | 1.40(10) |

Fit: Rwp 9.993%, Rp 7.102%, GOF 2.854. The final replay converged with maximum shift/esd 0.014, and `fit.csv` exactly matches the persisted project arrays.

